        <body>
            <div xml:id="d165">

                <pb facs="0427" n="337" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg337"/>

                <head>No. 161.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Avery to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-06-11"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 11, 1875</date>.
                        (Received August 9.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 63.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In further reference to the question of
                    missionary rights beyond treaty-ports, I have the honor to inclose an unofficial
                    note from R. M. Johnson, esq., consul at Han-kow, discussing a correspondence
                    between Mr. Beebe, formerly acting consular agent at Kiu-kiang, and Mr. Low, in
                    which the latter clearly states the views set forth at more length in his
                    dispatches to the Department.</p>
                <p>In my reply to Mr. Johnson I discourage the tendency to establish permanent
                    missions farther in the interior. It seems to be unnecessary to add anything to
                    what I have already written on the subject.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>BENJ. P. AVERY.</signed></closer>





